Hi Mika,=20

Thank you for the response (I honestly wasn't expecting anyone to respond f=
or this niche issue---so a welcome surprise).=20

Yes my BIOS version (v1.41.0) is up to date. Currently, there are no update=
s available through Windows or from fwupd. I have received BIOS updates man=
y times via Windows since purchasing this computer in 2022 and have had 0 c=
hange to this problem.=20

For what it=E2=80=99s worth, the DSDT has a valid default ID. TPD0 starts w=
ith Name (ITPN, =E2=80=9CDELL0AFF=E2=80=9D), and _INI then overwrites it fr=
om the BIOS-provided TPHD variable, which yields =E2=80=9CVEN_04F3=E2=80=
=9D.

Please find attached the output of 'sudo dmidecode'.=20

Thank you for helping me fix this.=20

=20

On Monday, November 9th, 2026 at 5:45 AM, Mika Westerberg <mika.westerberg@=
linux.intel.com> wrote:

> Hi,
>=20
> On Fri, Nov 06, 2026 at 05:48:02PM +0000, penhouse24@proton.me wrote:
> > Hi Mika, Andi,
> >
> > The ELAN 04F3:311C touchpad in the Dell XPS 17 9720 intermittently beco=
mes
> > sluggish and sticky for several seconds to minutes, then recovers. I du=
al boot
> > with Windows and I do not experience this issue on Windows, only on Lin=
ux and
> > across multiple distros (Linux mint, Ubuntu and Pop_os). I'm not the on=
ly one
> > who experiences this issue; it seems to be widespread among Dell XPS us=
ers.
> > This looks like the excessive-smoothing issue fixed for the Precision 5=
560 by
> > commit 0b7c9528facd (=E2=80=9Ci2c: Force DLL0945 touchpad i2c freq to 1=
00khz=E2=80=9D). The
> > Precision 5560 uses the same 04F3:311C touchpad. On the XPS 17 9720, th=
e
> > firmware exposes it with _HID =E2=80=9CVEN_04F3=E2=80=9D instead of =
=E2=80=9CDLL0945=E2=80=9D, so the existing
> > quirk doesn=E2=80=99t apply.
> >
> > Forcing the bus to 100 kHz fixes it here. The DSDT already supports 100=
 kHz:
> > _SB.PC00.I2C1.TPD0._INI sets SPED from the TPDS NVS variable (0 =3D 100=
 kHz, 1 =3D
> > 400 kHz, 2 =3D 1 MHz), and this firmware uses TPDS =3D 1. To test it, I=
 loaded an
> > SSDT through configfs that sets _SB.PC00.I2C1.TPD0.SPED =3D 0x186A0 and=
 rebound
> > i2c_designware.1. After 5 days of use, including on AC power, the lag h=
as not
> > come back. Without the override, it returns as randomly as before.
> >
> > System:
> >
> >   =E2=80=A2 Dell XPS 17 9720, BIOS 1.41.0
> >   =E2=80=A2 Touchpad: VEN_04F3:00 04F3:311C on i2c_designware.1 (_SB.PC=
00.I2C1.TPD0),
> >     touchpad firmware 0x000b
> >   =E2=80=A2 Kernel: 7.2.15-76070215-generic
> >
> > Adding =E2=80=9CVEN_04F3=E2=80=9D to i2c_acpi_force_100khz_device_ids w=
ould fix this machine.
> > However, VEN_04F3 looks like a generic vendor ID rather than a model-sp=
ecific
> > one like DLL0945, so it may need to be limited to this model with a DMI=
 match.
> > I=E2=80=99m happy to test a patch.
>=20
> That VEN_04F3 is not even a valid ACPI/PNP ID. Have you checked if there =
is
> a BIOS upgrade for your system?
>=20
> It's odd that Dell would use identifier like this as they typically follo=
w
> these pretty well. Anyways if there is no BIOS upgrade (or if it does not
> change anything) then I don't see much other option than adding a quirk. =
I
> do see there is one quirk added already for GPIO side on Dell system with
> VEN_0488 entry (drivers/gpio/gpiolib-acpi-quirks.c).
>=20
> In that case we would need output of 'sudo dmidecode' to be able to add t=
he
> DMI strings.
> 
